#
# prima-materia - the Maya ritual transmission
#
# Order of the file: the tradition, then the examination in the language of
# Zuyua and the office it guards, then the ordeals and the sign left behind in
# the Popol Vuh, then the fasts, the caution of the carvers and the remedy for
# a foretold year. The instance was registered in
# traditions/registry-outside-contact.ttl and is moved here now that passages
# have been read. Why it stands at a disputed contact route stands on the
# instance below, where it survives compilation.
#

pmt:MayaRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Maya ritual transmission"@en, "Maya-Ritualüberlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Maya transmission of creation, of the calendar as a moving structure of obligation, of divination by counting seeds, and of an examination in a figurative language by which the right to rule was said to be decided."@en ;
    skos:note "Registered at corpus named until this file was written, and moved here on the first passages actually read. What was read at the wording: the Chumayel, chapter IX, in the Roys translation; Landa in the Gates translation, sections XXVII, XXXVII and XL; the Popol Vuh in the Goetz and Morley version of the Recinos translation, part I chapter 2 and part II chapters 7 to 9. What was not opened: the Ritual of the Bacabs, the Dresden Codex, the Tizimin and Mani books, and Landa section XXVI. The reasons stand in the issues named in the harvest note of prima-materia#454."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route is entered as disputed and no longer as absent, and that correction is the finding of this run rather than a formality. Absent asks whether a route was available in the period in which the corpus was fixed, and every work read here was fixed after 1524: Landa's account survives only in a later copy, the Popol Vuh only in the hand of a Dominican of 1701 to 1703, the Chumayel in a compilation signed at Chumayel in 1782. That the Chilam Balam books received European material is not a suspicion but a philological result, demonstrated for the almanac and zodiac material of the Kaua book against named Spanish printed sources. Against that stands an argument for continuity in the incantation genres, which was not decided here. See prima-materia#455 and prima-materia#456."@en ;
    skos:note "A witness of this corpus is therefore a witness for the period of its fixation and not for the period it describes. The strongest case is Landa: the autograph of 1566 is lost, what survives is an anonymous copy, and the thesis that the surviving text is a compilation of the seventeenth or eighteenth century out of Landa's papers is in the literature and not answered here. See prima-materia#457."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Popol Vuh in the Ximenez manuscript; the Books of Chilam Balam; the Dresden Codex; the Ritual of the Bacabs; Landa, Relacion de las cosas de Yucatan, as the account of an outside observer" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    dcterms:source "Ralph L. Roys, The Book of Chilam Balam of Chumayel (Norman: University of Oklahoma Press, 1967), chapter IX, pp. 89-98" .

